# Fixture bytes are the contract.
#
# `cargo run -p xtask -- verify-fixtures` checks every file under `fixtures/`
# against a sha256 and size recorded in `fixtures/manifest.json`, and the byte
# gates compare `.lvi`/`.lsi`/`.lsb` output against these files exactly. Git on
# Windows defaults to `core.autocrlf=true`, which rewrites every LF on checkout —
# adding one byte per line and changing the hash of anything it touches. That
# broke the Windows lane on the JSON manifests and `chunker.input` while leaving
# the code untouched, which makes it look like a data problem rather than a
# checkout one.
#
# `-text` disables end-of-line conversion in both directions. Preferred over
# `binary` (which is `-text -diff`) so the JSON manifests still diff readably.
fixtures/** -text
